WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.760
- We're not going to do
a few examples questions

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.470
from the Khan academy exercise

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.680
on the standard algorithms.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.290
We're asked, which of the following

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:12.080
correctly multiplies 74 times 8

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.610
using the standard algorithm.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.140
So pause this video and
see if you can work on that

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.410
before we do it together.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.290
All right, now let's just remind ourselves

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:23.720
what the standard algorithm is.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.090
In fact, let's just remind
ourselves what an algorithm is.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.040
An algorithm is a series
of steps that you can do

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.930
to do something, so
you'll often hear about

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.300
a computer algorithm.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.070
But you can also have algorithms in math,

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.240
just a method for doing something.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.590
And the standard algorithm,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.830
that's the typical, or the standard, way

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:43.450
that a lot of people will tackle

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.260
a multiplication question
or computation like this.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.850
But just as a reminder,
in the standard algorithm

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.460
if we're multiplying 74 times 8

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.340
we would write the eight in the ones place

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.590
right below the four in the ones place,

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.280
and then you multiply each of
these places times the eight.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.840
So you would start with
the four times the eight,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.740
you would get 32.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:11.270
32 you can express as
two ones and three tens.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.090
So we'll put that three up there.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:17.830
And then you would multiple
the seven times the eight.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:22.830
Seven times eight is 56, and
that's going to be 56 tens

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.410
because it's seven 10s
times eight is 56 tens.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.780
Plus the three 10s you had before

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.350
get you to 59 tens, and so
you would write over here.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:38.990
That's 59 tens, and so this would be 592.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:40.150
Now when you look at the choices,

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.860
that's exactly what
happened here in choice C.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.240
Just for kicks we can see what went wrong

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.073
in these other ones.

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:48.360
Let's see, in this first one,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.700
when we multiplied the,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.060
when we multiplied the
four ones times eight ones,

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.260
according to this, this person somehow got

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.230
three ones and two tens, and 23.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:03.150
Four times eight is not 23,
you can rule that one out.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.730
And here when they multiplied

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:07.670
the four ones times the eight ones,

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:10.090
that would be the 32.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:14.470
So it's two ones and
then another three tens,

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.580
so there should've been a three up here.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.260
And so that way when you multiplied

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.390
the seven tens times eight,

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.760
you get 56 but then you
add this other three tens

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.375
so you would really
need to get to 59 tens.

00:02:24.375 --> 00:02:27.030
So that's why that one didn't work.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.090
Let's do another example here.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.500
And this is going to be with
a different type of question.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.360
So here we are told that,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.630
Don starts to use the standard algorithm

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.800
to solve 418 times five.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.030
His work is shown below.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.160
What number should Don replace Y with?

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.493
Pause this video and see
if you can figure it out.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.040
Okay, so the way to think about this,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.750
this might at first
confuse you a little bit,

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:56.150
because Y, and why is there a
Y there, in the first place?

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.610
But what they're really trying to get at

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:02.310
is making sure that you, or we, understand

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.760
what Don is trying to do when

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.560
he's trying to do the standard algorithms.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.210
So as we just highlighted in last example,

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:09.360
the way that we would tackle this

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.420
with the standard algorithm,

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.610
and actually let me write
it down, 418 times five.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:18.030
We would say eight ones times five ones

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.850
is going to be 40 ones.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:25.720
40 ones we can write as
zero ones and four tens.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.230
And so that looks like
the place where they,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.010
where Don stopped computing.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:32.210
So he's on his way to
solving the whole thing

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.680
he just partially computed it so far.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.210
But just by doing that, we
know what the Y should be.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.770
The Y should be four.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.240
So what number should
Don replace the Y with?

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.960
He should replace it with a four,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.420
and it's representing
Y tens, or four tens.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.470
And of course you could keep
going with this computation.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:55.930
If Don were to say, okay,
I have one ten times five,

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.470
that'd be five tens,
plus another four tens,

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.080
that's nine tens.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.680
And then last but not least,

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.930
if Don wanted to figure out, well,

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.710
he's got four hundreds here times five

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.220
is twenty hundreds,

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.670
which we can express as zero
hundreds and two thousands.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:14.940
Or you just do this as 20 hundreds.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.550
So they're not asking us to
do the entire computation.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:18.420
We're just trying to figure out

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.690
what Don did essentially
in this first step.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.740
What number should he have
written here instead of a Y?

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.790
So Y could be replaced with a four.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.420
It's representing the four,

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.820
which is really in the
tens place, so four tens.

